    "q-48106": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48106",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -x, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $f(1)=1,$ so $f(1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-48106-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist",
